    {
      "id": "adr-automerge",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Renovate merges patch and minor updates on its own",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 0,
      "concern": "dependency-hygiene",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "This element is built from third-party packages that keep moving whether or not we follow. The forces: a dependency left alone for a year is upgraded in one painful jump, with every breaking change at once, while small frequent updates each cost minutes; a bot that merges on its own needs tests worth trusting, and one that only opens pull requests buries the team under them; a manifest with ranges and no lockfile builds something different each week, while exact pins turn every update into a commit someone has to make; a package nobody imports any more still gets installed, scanned and patched, and one whose maintainer left stops getting fixes without telling anyone; and a licence that forbids what we do with the code is found at the worst time, in due diligence. Vulnerability scanning, integrity hashes, where packages are fetched from, SBOMs and build provenance are decided by the Software supply chain concern; this one decides how the dependencies are kept current, pinned, used and licensed.",
      "decision": "Renovate (the Mend-hosted GitHub app, or the self-hosted runner) opens one pull request per dependency update from renovate.json in the repository. Patch and minor updates of packages at 1.0.0 or above merge automatically once the required checks pass and the release has been public for 3 days (minimumReleaseAge); major updates and updates of 0.x packages get a label and wait for a reviewer. Lockfile maintenance runs weekly. Pinning: {{PINNING}}. Unused dependencies: {{UNUSED}}. Licences: {{LICENCES}}. Freshness: {{AGE}}. Deprecated or unreleased-for-two-years dependencies are tracked and replaced within {{REVIEW_CADENCE_DAYS}} days. Renovate over Dependabot here because Dependabot has no automerge of its own and no minimum release age.",
      "consequences": "• Most updates land the day after release, in steps small enough that a failing one is easy to read.\n• The build is the only reviewer of a minor update: it has to be one the team would release on (Test strategy concern), or automerge is a way to ship breakage unattended.\n• A package that breaks semantic versioning in a minor release can still get through; the minimum release age and the tests are the defence, and such a package gets a packageRule that turns automerge off.\n• Renovate needs the app installed and permission to merge; the dependency dashboard issue is where paused and rate-limited updates show up.",
      "alternatives": "1. One weekly grouped pull request a person merges: more control, more drift, one more chore a week.\n2. Dependabot with automerge through a GitHub Actions workflow: works, but the merge logic lives in a workflow of our own and there is no minimum release age.\n3. Manual updates from an outdated report: fine for a frozen codebase, a year of drift for a living one.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "UPDATES": [
          "automerge-patch"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-weekly-batch",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "One grouped update pull request a week",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 130,
      "concern": "dependency-hygiene",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "This element is built from third-party packages that keep moving whether or not we follow. The forces: a dependency left alone for a year is upgraded in one painful jump, with every breaking change at once, while small frequent updates each cost minutes; a bot that merges on its own needs tests worth trusting, and one that only opens pull requests buries the team under them; a manifest with ranges and no lockfile builds something different each week, while exact pins turn every update into a commit someone has to make; a package nobody imports any more still gets installed, scanned and patched, and one whose maintainer left stops getting fixes without telling anyone; and a licence that forbids what we do with the code is found at the worst time, in due diligence. Vulnerability scanning, integrity hashes, where packages are fetched from, SBOMs and build provenance are decided by the Software supply chain concern; this one decides how the dependencies are kept current, pinned, used and licensed.",
      "decision": "An update bot opens one pull request every {{UPDATE_DAY}} with all pending patch and minor updates grouped, and a separate one per major update: Dependabot version updates with groups in .github/dependabot.yml on GitHub, or Renovate with a single groupName and a weekly schedule elsewhere. A person reviews the batch, reads the changelogs of anything that touches the public behaviour, and merges or closes it within 5 working days; nothing merges without the required checks. Pinning: {{PINNING}}. Unused dependencies: {{UNUSED}}. Licences: {{LICENCES}}. Freshness: {{AGE}}. Deprecated or unreleased-for-two-years dependencies are tracked and replaced within {{REVIEW_CADENCE_DAYS}} days.",
      "consequences": "• A week of drift at most, and the team sees what changed.\n• One batch is bisected by hand when it breaks: with many updates in it, finding the culprit takes a while, which is why majors stay separate.\n• The batch needs an owner each week or it goes stale and the next one conflicts with it.\n• Dependabot groups cannot merge on their own; moving to automerge later means moving to Renovate or adding a workflow.",
      "alternatives": "1. Automerge of patch and minor updates with Renovate: less drift and no weekly chore, but the tests must be trusted.\n2. One pull request per update, unmerged: dozens of open pull requests that nobody reads.\n3. Manual updates from an outdated report: the same drift with more work.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "UPDATES": [
          "weekly-batch"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-manual",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Dependencies updated by hand on a cadence",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 260,
      "concern": "dependency-hygiene",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "This element is built from third-party packages that keep moving whether or not we follow. The forces: a dependency left alone for a year is upgraded in one painful jump, with every breaking change at once, while small frequent updates each cost minutes; a bot that merges on its own needs tests worth trusting, and one that only opens pull requests buries the team under them; a manifest with ranges and no lockfile builds something different each week, while exact pins turn every update into a commit someone has to make; a package nobody imports any more still gets installed, scanned and patched, and one whose maintainer left stops getting fixes without telling anyone; and a licence that forbids what we do with the code is found at the worst time, in due diligence. Vulnerability scanning, integrity hashes, where packages are fetched from, SBOMs and build provenance are decided by the Software supply chain concern; this one decides how the dependencies are kept current, pinned, used and licensed.",
      "decision": "No bot opens pull requests. A scheduled job runs every {{MANUAL_CADENCE_DAYS}} days, produces the outdated report (npm outdated --json, pip list --outdated --format=json, go list -m -u all) and opens an issue with it; a person updates or explicitly defers every line in one reviewed change before the next report, and the job fails if the previous report's issue is still open. Pinning: {{PINNING}}. Unused dependencies: {{UNUSED}}. Licences: {{LICENCES}}. Freshness: {{AGE}}. Deprecated or unreleased-for-two-years dependencies are tracked and replaced within {{REVIEW_CADENCE_DAYS}} days. This decision fits a small or frozen codebase; when the element returns to active development it is superseded by a bot.",
      "consequences": "• No bot, no app permissions, no stream of pull requests.\n• Up to {{MANUAL_CADENCE_DAYS}} days of drift, and the update is one larger change rather than many small ones.\n• Depends on a person doing the chore; the failing report job is the only thing that notices when they do not.\n• Security fixes in dependencies wait for the cadence unless the Software supply chain concern's deadlines pull them forward.",
      "alternatives": "1. Renovate with automerge of patch and minor updates: continuous, needs trusted tests.\n2. A weekly grouped bot pull request: the same cadence with the diff prepared for you; preferable wherever a bot may run.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "UPDATES": [
          "manual"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-renovate-rules",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Renovate automerges only safe updates",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 0,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "renovate.json validates; patch and minor updates of packages at 1.0.0 or above automerge after 3 days, 0 major or 0.x updates automerge; OpenSSF Scorecard Dependency-Update-Tool scores 10",
      "description": "The repository's renovate.json is validated in CI with renovate-config-validator, and its package rules are what decide the gate: automerge for patch and minor updates of stable packages, a minimum release age of 3 days, majors labelled for a person. Scorecard's Dependency-Update-Tool check passes once the file is present.\n\nTool: Renovate (renovate-config-validator in CI)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json\",\n  \"extends\": [\"config:recommended\"],\n  \"minimumReleaseAge\": \"3 days\",\n  \"lockFileMaintenance\": { \"enabled\": true, \"schedule\": [\"before 6am on monday\"] },\n  \"packageRules\": [\n    { \"matchUpdateTypes\": [\"patch\", \"minor\"], \"matchCurrentVersion\": \"!/^0/\",\n      \"automerge\": true, \"automergeType\": \"pr\", \"platformAutomerge\": true },\n    { \"matchUpdateTypes\": [\"major\"], \"automerge\": false, \"labels\": [\"major-update\"] },\n    { \"matchCurrentVersion\": \"/^0/\", \"automerge\": false, \"labels\": [\"pre-1.0\"] }\n  ]\n}\n// CI: npx --package renovate renovate-config-validator renovate.json\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "UPDATES": [
          "automerge-patch"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-weekly-groups",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Updates grouped weekly",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 130,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "At most 1 open pull request with patch and minor updates at a time, opened on {{UPDATE_DAY}}; 0 update pull requests open longer than 7 days; OpenSSF Scorecard Dependency-Update-Tool scores 10",
      "description": "Dependabot groups every patch and minor update into one weekly pull request and keeps majors separate; a daily job fails when an update pull request is older than 7 days. With Renovate, the same is one packageRule with groupName \"weekly updates\" and schedule [\"before 6am on {{UPDATE_DAY}}\"].\n\nTool: Dependabot version updates (grouped) with the GitHub CLI\n\n```yaml\n# .github/dependabot.yml\nversion: 2\nupdates:\n  - package-ecosystem: npm\n    directory: /\n    schedule: { interval: weekly, day: {{UPDATE_DAY}}, time: \"06:00\" }\n    open-pull-requests-limit: 5\n    groups:\n      weekly-updates:\n        patterns: [\"*\"]\n        update-types: [\"minor\", \"patch\"]\n# daily: no update pull request older than 7 days\n# gh pr list --author app/dependabot --state open --json createdAt \\\n#   | jq -e '[.[] | select((now - (.createdAt | fromdateiso8601)) > 7 * 86400)] | length == 0'\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "UPDATES": [
          "weekly-batch"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-outdated-report",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Outdated report on schedule",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 260,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "A report issue is opened every {{MANUAL_CADENCE_DAYS}} days; 0 report issues still open when the next one is due",
      "description": "A scheduled workflow runs npm outdated --json, turns it into a table and opens an issue labelled dependencies; before opening it fails when the previous report is still open, which is the signal that the update did not happen.\n\nTool: npm outdated with the GitHub CLI (GitHub Actions schedule)\n\n```bash\n# runs on a schedule every MANUAL_CADENCE_DAYS days (cron: '0 6 1,15 * *' for 14)\nopen=$(gh issue list --label dependencies --state open --json number --jq 'length')\n[ \"$open\" -eq 0 ] || { echo \"the previous dependency report is still open\"; exit 1; }\nnpm outdated --json > outdated.json || true           # exits 1 when something is outdated\n{\n  echo \"| package | current | wanted | latest |\"; echo \"|---|---|---|---|\"\n  jq -r 'to_entries[] | \"| \\(.key) | \\(.value.current) | \\(.value.wanted) | \\(.value.latest) |\"' outdated.json\n} > report.md\ngh issue create --title \"Dependency update $(date +%F)\" --label dependencies --body-file report.md\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "UPDATES": [
          "manual"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-lockfile-frozen",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Install refuses lockfile drift",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 390,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 builds in which the install changes the lockfile or a manifest has no lockfile beside it",
      "description": "CI installs with the frozen-lockfile command of each package manager and fails when the lockfile would change or is missing. Elsewhere: pnpm install --frozen-lockfile, uv sync --locked, poetry check --lock, go mod tidy -diff, dotnet restore --locked-mode. The lockfile's integrity hashes and where packages come from are the Software supply chain concern.\n\nTool: npm ci with git diff (GitHub Actions)\n\n```bash\nfor manifest in $(git ls-files '*package.json' | grep -v node_modules); do\n  dir=$(dirname \"$manifest\")\n  [ -f \"$dir/package-lock.json\" ] || { echo \"$dir has no lockfile\"; exit 1; }\ndone\nnpm ci --ignore-scripts                     # fails when package.json and the lockfile disagree\ngit diff --exit-code -- '*package-lock.json' # and nothing may have rewritten it\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "PINNING": [
          "lockfile-ranges",
          "exact"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-exact-versions",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Manifest holds exact versions",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 520,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 dependencies declared with a range; save-exact is set so new installs stay exact",
      "description": "A unit test reads the manifest and fails on any dependency whose version is a caret, tilde, wildcard, comparison or hyphen range; .npmrc sets save-exact so that npm install writes exact versions. Python: pin with == in pyproject; Go modules are exact by construction.\n\nTool: Vitest (a test over package.json) and .npmrc save-exact\n\n```ts\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\n\nconst pkg = JSON.parse(readFileSync('package.json', 'utf8'))\nconst RANGE = /^[\\^~]|[<>=xX*]|\\|\\||\\s-\\s/\nconst ranged = Object.entries({ ...pkg.dependencies, ...pkg.devDependencies })\n  .filter(([, version]) => RANGE.test(String(version)))\n\nit('every dependency is declared with one exact version', () => {\n  expect(ranged).toEqual([])\n})\n// .npmrc: save-exact=true\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "PINNING": [
          "exact"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-range-matrix",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Ranges tested at both ends",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 650,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "The tests pass with the lowest and with the highest resolution of the declared ranges; 0 lockfiles tracked in the repository",
      "description": "A pull-request job fails when a lockfile is tracked, then installs the library twice, once resolving every direct dependency to the lowest version its range allows and once to the highest, and runs the tests on each. For a JavaScript library the same is pnpm install --config.resolution-mode=lowest-direct against the default.\n\nTool: uv (resolution lowest-direct and highest) in GitHub Actions\n\n```yaml\n# .github/workflows/range.yml\njobs:\n  range:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    strategy: { matrix: { resolution: [lowest-direct, highest] } }\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: |\n          tracked=$(git ls-files uv.lock poetry.lock package-lock.json pnpm-lock.yaml yarn.lock)\n          [ -z \"$tracked\" ] || { echo \"a library commits no lockfile: $tracked\"; exit 1; }\n      - uses: astral-sh/setup-uv@v5\n      - run: uv pip install --system --resolution ${{ matrix.resolution }} -e '.[test]'\n      - run: pytest\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "PINNING": [
          "floating"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-knip",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Unused and unlisted dependencies fail",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 780,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 unused dependencies, 0 unlisted imports, 0 unresolved imports on every pull request",
      "description": "knip runs as a required check with dependency findings as errors; files and exports stay warnings until the backlog is clean. Python: deptry . with DEP001 to DEP004 in pyproject [tool.deptry]; Go: go mod tidy -diff fails when go.mod would change.\n\nTool: knip (deptry for Python, go mod tidy -diff for Go)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://unpkg.com/knip@5/schema.json\",\n  \"entry\": [\"src/index.ts\", \"src/cli.ts\"],\n  \"project\": [\"src/**/*.ts\"],\n  \"ignoreDependencies\": [],\n  \"rules\": {\n    \"dependencies\": \"error\",\n    \"devDependencies\": \"error\",\n    \"unlisted\": \"error\",\n    \"unresolved\": \"error\",\n    \"files\": \"warn\",\n    \"exports\": \"warn\"\n  }\n}\n// CI: npx knip --reporter compact\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "UNUSED": [
          "forbidden"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-unused-review",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Unused report is acted on",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 910,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "A report issue every {{REVIEW_CADENCE_DAYS}} days; 0 packages named in two consecutive reports without a reason in dependency-exceptions.json",
      "description": "A scheduled workflow runs knip in report mode, opens the report as an issue, and fails when a package named in the previous report is named again without an entry in dependency-exceptions.json.\n\nTool: knip (JSON reporter) with jq and the GitHub CLI\n\n```bash\n# scheduled every REVIEW_CADENCE_DAYS days (cron: '0 6 1 */3 *' for 90)\nnpx knip --reporter json --include dependencies,devDependencies,unlisted > unused.json || true\njq -r '.issues[] | .dependencies[]?.name, .devDependencies[]?.name' unused.json | sort -u > now.txt\ngh issue list --label unused-dependencies --state all --limit 1 --json body --jq '.[0].body' | grep -oE '^- \\S+' | cut -c3- | sort -u > before.txt\njq -r '.[].package' dependency-exceptions.json | sort -u > excused.txt\ncomm -12 now.txt before.txt | comm -23 - excused.txt > repeat.txt\n{ echo \"Unused dependencies as of $(date +%F):\"; sed 's/^/- /' now.txt; } > report.md\ngh issue create --title \"Unused dependencies $(date +%F)\" --label unused-dependencies --body-file report.md\ntest ! -s repeat.txt || { echo \"named twice without a reason:\"; cat repeat.txt; exit 1; }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "UNUSED": [
          "reviewed"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-licence-allowlist",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Licences on the allowlist",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1040,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "100 % of production dependencies carry one of {{ALLOWED_LICENCES}}; the build fails on the first that does not",
      "description": "The licence checker of each ecosystem runs on every pull request with the allowlist and exits non-zero on the first package outside it, unknown licences included. pip-licenses matches the names packages declare, so its list is kept in those names next to the SPDX one.\n\nTool: license-checker-rseidelsohn (npm), pip-licenses, go-licenses\n\n```bash\nALLOWED=\"{{ALLOWED_LICENCES}}\"        # SPDX identifiers\n# npm: production tree, first licence outside the list exits 1\nnpx license-checker-rseidelsohn --production --excludePrivatePackages --onlyAllow \"${ALLOWED//, /;}\"\n# Go\ngo-licenses check ./... --allowed_licenses=\"${ALLOWED// /}\"\n# Python: names as packages declare them (pip-licenses prints them)\npip-licenses --allow-only \"MIT License;Apache Software License;BSD License;ISC License (ISCL)\"\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "LICENCES": [
          "allowlist"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-licence-denylist",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Forbidden licences refused",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1170,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 production dependencies carrying one of {{DENIED_LICENCES}}; every other licence listed in the build report",
      "description": "The licence checker runs on every pull request with the denylist, fails on the first match and writes the full licence list as a build artefact for the record.\n\nTool: license-checker-rseidelsohn (npm), pip-licenses, go-licenses\n\n```bash\nDENIED=\"{{DENIED_LICENCES}}\"          # SPDX identifiers\n# npm: exits 1 on the first package with a forbidden licence; the report lists the rest\nnpx license-checker-rseidelsohn --production --csv --out reports/licences.csv --failOn \"${DENIED//, /;}\"\n# Go: forbidden = strong copyleft in go-licenses' classification\ngo-licenses check ./... --disallowed_types=forbidden\n# Python\npip-licenses --fail-on \"GNU General Public License v3 (GPLv3);GNU Affero General Public License v3\"\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "LICENCES": [
          "denylist"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-libyear",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Freshness within bounds",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1300,
      "category": "operational",
      "threshold": "Direct dependencies at most {{MAX_LIBYEARS}} libyears behind in total; no single one more than {{MAX_MONTHS_BEHIND}} months behind",
      "description": "libyear runs on every pull request and fails when the collective drift or any dependency's own drift crosses the bound, naming the dependencies behind. Python: the libyear package prints the total per requirements file; compare it in the job.\n\nTool: libyear (npm) and libyear (PyPI)\n\n```bash\n# npm: fails above either bound and lists the dependencies behind\nnpx libyear \\\n  --threshold-drift-collective {{MAX_LIBYEARS}} \\\n  --threshold-drift-individual \"$(awk 'BEGIN { print {{MAX_MONTHS_BEHIND}} / 12 }')\"\n# Python\ntotal=$(libyear -r requirements.txt | awk '/libyears/ { print $(NF-1) }')\nawk -v t=\"$total\" 'BEGIN { exit (t > {{MAX_LIBYEARS}}) }' || { echo \"$total libyears behind\"; exit 1; }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "AGE": [
          "max-age"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-abandoned",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "No deprecated or dormant dependency",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1430,
      "category": "operational",
      "threshold": "0 direct dependencies marked deprecated on the registry or without a release for 24 months, unless listed with a ticket in dependency-exceptions.json",
      "description": "A daily job asks the registry about every direct dependency and fails on one that is deprecated or whose last release is older than 24 months; each hit gets an issue and a deadline of {{REVIEW_CADENCE_DAYS}} days. Python: pip index versions and the PyPI JSON API; Go: the module's latest version date from go list -m -u -json.\n\nTool: npm view with jq (GitHub Actions schedule)\n\n```bash\njq -r '(.dependencies + .devDependencies) | keys[]' package.json | while read -r pkg; do\n  npm view \"$pkg\" --json | jq -r --arg pkg \"$pkg\" '\n    ((now - (.time.modified | fromdateiso8601)) / 86400 | floor) as $days\n    | if .deprecated then \"\\($pkg): deprecated: \\(.deprecated)\"\n      elif $days > 730 then \"\\($pkg): last release \\($days) days ago\"\n      else empty end'\ndone | grep -v -F -f <(jq -r '.[].package' dependency-exceptions.json) | tee abandoned.txt\ntest ! -s abandoned.txt   # each line gets an issue with a {{REVIEW_CADENCE_DAYS}}-day deadline\n```"
    }
  ],
